## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of policy-driven routing and privacy in a beginner-friendly way, focusing on practical data flow.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped policies
- Ingress routing with model selection
- Per-tenant redaction rules
- Observability and audit trails

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: route based on tenant policy
function route(req, policyStore){
  const p = policyStore.get(req.tenantId);
  if(!p) throw new Error('no policy');
  const m = p.allowedModels.includes(req.dataSensitivity) ? 'ModelA' : 'ModelB';
  // apply redaction if needed, then forward to m
  return m;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test latency guarantees across tenants?
- How would you handle policy drift when tenants update their settings?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes how to architect robust, scalable, and private multi-tenant routing for LLMs with shared infrastructure, covering isolation, governance, and latency.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant adapters
- Data locality and provenance
- Drift detection and safety gates
- Latency SLAs and auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-routing sketch
function route(request, tenant) {
  const adapter = getAdapter(tenant);
  const tagged = tagProvenance(request, tenant);
  if(!policyAllows(tagged, adapter)) throw new Error("Forbidden");
  return adapter.forwardToModel(tagged);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
